MEMORY MAP AND SAFE INSPECTION

Address ranges depend on Pico vs Pico W and current build. Do not
assume the example addresses always match the running image.
Typical RP2040/Pico W layout used by PicoNIX:
  0x20000000..0x2000dfff  kernel region (56 KiB)
  0x2000e000..0x20031fff  six user slots x 24 KiB
  Remaining areas include shared memory, RAM files, trace and
  network reservation. Confirm the actual build report.

INITIAL READ-ONLY CHECK
  cat /proc/memory
  cat /proc/ramfiles
  ps
  lsof
  ls /proc/sym
  grep pwm /proc/sym/kernel

BOUNDED RAW READ
  hexdump /dev/mem 0x2000e000 64
  hexdump /dev/mem 0x2000e040 64
Never read arbitrary memory unless the address is known valid.
A memory dump is not a license to write to /dev/mem.

FILE DUMPS VS RAW MEMORY
  hexdump /tmp/a.out 0 64
  hexdump /dev/shm 0 64
  hexdump /dev/shm 0 128
Use explicit offset and count. A 512-byte binary dump expands to
much more than 512 text bytes. MCP output limit is 4096 bytes.

SYMBOLS
  grep pwm /proc/sym/kernel
  mem -s kernel
'mem -s' prints symbol list, but interactive 'mem' starts a
full-screen viewer: do not use it through MCP. Kernel symbols may
have XIP flash or SRAM addresses; inspect the prefix/address.

INTERPRETING RESULTS
  00 bytes: not proof that memory is unused or uninitialized.
  changing values: may be timer, stack, ring buffer or concurrency.
  fixed addresses: need the current ELF/map to attribute precisely.
  a heap or stack overrun can corrupt adjacent data before failure.

Reserved SRAM area size is not current consumption. A 56-KiB
kernel allocation does NOT imply 56 KiB actually in use.
ps reports a time-specific snapshot; short-lived sh and commands
may disappear when execute_command returns. See process-resources.
